<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nahe River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nahe is a left tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, about 125 km long, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a> and joining the Rhine at Bingen. Its valley, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> and the North Palatinate uplands, passes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idar-oberstein">Idar-Oberstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-kreuznach">Bad Kreuznach</a> and is known for its wines. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was divided among the counts of Sponheim, the Wild- and Rhinegraves and the Palatinate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahe.jpg" alt="Nahe" width="320" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lantus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Nahe_Einzugsgebiet.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
